00:07:38.500 --> 00:07:40.870
So I'm going to cover
four categories where

00:07:40.870 --> 00:07:44.260
Cloud Dataprep is either new
or significantly improved

00:07:44.260 --> 00:07:46.960
in both the existing product
as well as the new standard

00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:48.880
and premium editions.

00:07:48.880 --> 00:07:50.740
So the first area
we're going to cover

00:07:50.740 --> 00:07:52.900
is our new self-service
connectors,

00:07:52.900 --> 00:07:55.450
which allow us to easily
access all your data

00:07:55.450 --> 00:07:57.790
from within Dataprep itself.

00:07:57.790 --> 00:08:01.270
While native GCP connectivity
to various sources and targets

00:08:01.270 --> 00:08:03.430
has always been there,
you don't always

00:08:03.430 --> 00:08:06.040
have your initial
starting point of data

00:08:06.040 --> 00:08:08.080
in Cloud Storage or BigQuery.

00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:11.140
A common example of
this is Google Sheets,

00:08:11.140 --> 00:08:14.740
which businesses use as a
primary method for collecting

00:08:14.740 --> 00:08:16.210
and sharing data.

00:08:16.210 --> 00:08:18.640
Typically, they want to
combine this data with tables

00:08:18.640 --> 00:08:23.260
in BigQuery or standardize it
against files in Cloud Storage.

00:08:23.260 --> 00:08:25.180
In this case, Cloud
Dataprep's goal

00:08:25.180 --> 00:08:27.700
is to provide the same
familiar search experience

00:08:27.700 --> 00:08:30.310
that Google Drive has
to these same users

00:08:30.310 --> 00:08:31.980
so they can easily
find and import

00:08:31.980 --> 00:08:34.179
that data set
directly into Dataprep

00:08:34.179 --> 00:08:36.909
without any additional
configuration.

00:08:36.909 --> 00:08:40.120
As a bonus, there is no
limit to the size of data

00:08:40.120 --> 00:08:42.520
or the number of Sheets
that you can import.

00:08:42.520 --> 00:08:45.580
For example, in cases where
there are new Sheets being

00:08:45.580 --> 00:08:48.760
added to a single G-suite
document every day,

00:08:48.760 --> 00:08:51.340
you can configure Cloud Dataprep
to automatically pick up

00:08:51.340 --> 00:08:54.160
all these new Sheets and add
them to the existing data

00:08:54.160 --> 00:08:58.280
source without having to make
any changes to your recipe.

00:08:58.280 --> 00:09:00.770
In other cases, users
need quick access

00:09:00.770 --> 00:09:02.750
to a handful of
sources typically found

00:09:02.750 --> 00:09:04.380
in traditional databases.

00:09:04.380 --> 00:09:06.320
So either to do
a lookup or maybe

00:09:06.320 --> 00:09:08.630
fetch the latest
delta of data that's

00:09:08.630 --> 00:09:11.130
available in the
database itself.

00:09:11.130 --> 00:09:13.380
This is where the new
relational connectors,

00:09:13.380 --> 00:09:15.980
such as Oracle, SQL
server, [INAUDIBLE],,

00:09:15.980 --> 00:09:19.130
just to name a few, come in
handy, as well as application

00:09:19.130 --> 00:09:21.830
connectors to
Salesforce, make it easy

00:09:21.830 --> 00:09:23.900
for you to grab all
the necessary data

00:09:23.900 --> 00:09:26.870
from a single interface by
just simply selecting from one

00:09:26.870 --> 00:09:29.700
of the existing connections.

00:09:29.700 --> 00:09:31.830
Last but not least,
BigQuery continues

00:09:31.830 --> 00:09:34.260
to be the primary source
and target for a majority

00:09:34.260 --> 00:09:35.770
of the Dataprep users.

00:09:35.770 --> 00:09:37.710
And we continue to
make key improvements

00:09:37.710 --> 00:09:41.310
in this area, such as automatic
column filtering, which

00:09:41.310 --> 00:09:43.740
is a huge performance
gain, because what it does

00:09:43.740 --> 00:09:45.810
is automatically prune
some of the columns

00:09:45.810 --> 00:09:47.790
that you aren't
using so you process

00:09:47.790 --> 00:09:51.030
less data and the whole
result comes back faster.

00:09:51.030 --> 00:09:54.270
On the flexibility side, we
now allow custom SQL data

00:09:54.270 --> 00:09:57.660
sets, which means users can
create their own standard SQL

00:09:57.660 --> 00:10:00.840
statement, and actually
import that and use

00:10:00.840 --> 00:10:02.290
that as a data source itself.

00:10:02.290 --> 00:10:02.790
OK.

00:10:02.790 --> 00:10:04.123
Let's move on to the next slide.

00:10:04.123 --> 00:10:06.270
As users build longer
recipes and continue

00:10:06.270 --> 00:10:08.310
to combine more
data sources, we've

00:10:08.310 --> 00:10:11.520
seen the size and complexity of
their Dataprep flows increase

00:10:11.520 --> 00:10:12.780
dramatically.

00:10:12.780 --> 00:10:14.910
Thanks to vocal feedback
from our community,

00:10:14.910 --> 00:10:18.180
we've revamped the flow UI
for improved productivity.

00:10:18.180 --> 00:10:20.670
Now you can drag and drop
your recipes, your sources,

00:10:20.670 --> 00:10:23.400
and even your outputs,
into customized layouts.

00:10:23.400 --> 00:10:26.190
And you have easy access
to common transformations

00:10:26.190 --> 00:10:29.550
like union or join
directly from the flow.

00:10:29.550 --> 00:10:32.580
The addition of email
notifications and webhook tasks

00:10:32.580 --> 00:10:34.860
allow you to
configure who and how

00:10:34.860 --> 00:10:37.860
you want other users or
services to be alerted when

00:10:37.860 --> 00:10:40.170
Dataprep jobs are completed.

00:10:40.170 --> 00:10:42.270
This can be configured
to start immediately

00:10:42.270 --> 00:10:44.550
after a job has been
completed successfully,

00:10:44.550 --> 00:10:45.858
where you can send an email.

00:10:45.858 --> 00:10:47.400
Or, in the case of
failure, maybe you

00:10:47.400 --> 00:10:49.560
need to notify a
down train system.

00:10:49.560 --> 00:10:51.600
Whatever use case
best fits your needs,

00:10:51.600 --> 00:10:54.370
we should be able
to support that.

00:10:54.370 --> 00:10:55.790
A big development
is the addition

00:10:55.790 --> 00:10:57.890
of recipe parameters,
which allow

00:10:57.890 --> 00:11:00.530
you to make a recipe
behave dynamically

00:11:00.530 --> 00:11:04.850
based on inputs that are
inserted at job execution time.

00:11:04.850 --> 00:11:07.610
This is critical for
sharing and reusability,

00:11:07.610 --> 00:11:10.640
where one user creates a
common piece of logic that

00:11:10.640 --> 00:11:12.770
can be used by many others.

00:11:12.770 --> 00:11:15.290
Users can achieve this
by defining recipe steps

00:11:15.290 --> 00:11:19.040
with parameters that change
the logic based on the input.

00:11:19.040 --> 00:11:21.560
A common example is
financial calculations,

00:11:21.560 --> 00:11:23.660
which often need the
latest currency conversion

00:11:23.660 --> 00:11:26.872
rates to be passed in
at the time of run.

00:11:26.872 --> 00:11:28.580
And so now you can do
that without having

00:11:28.580 --> 00:11:31.250
to modify the recipe at all.

00:11:31.250 --> 00:11:34.080
And last thing to note, in
order to round out reusability,

00:11:34.080 --> 00:11:35.850
we've made it much
easier to import

00:11:35.850 --> 00:11:38.310
export flows across projects.

00:11:38.310 --> 00:11:41.550
This makes it much easier to
jumpstart a new initiative

00:11:41.550 --> 00:11:45.120
by simply migrating a copy
of your flows and recipes

00:11:45.120 --> 00:11:47.370
to another user so they
can start getting started

00:11:47.370 --> 00:11:50.110
with Dataprep from there.

00:11:50.110 --> 00:11:52.930
Flow by itself is
not always enough.

00:11:52.930 --> 00:11:56.350
Sometimes you might need to
chain together multiple flows.

00:11:56.350 --> 00:11:58.930
Or you need to combine
them with external services

00:11:58.930 --> 00:12:01.660
so you can achieve your
end to end use case.

00:12:01.660 --> 00:12:05.170
That's why we're introducing
a brand new visual interface,

00:12:05.170 --> 00:12:07.810
Cloud Dataprep Plans,
that orchestrate

00:12:07.810 --> 00:12:10.300
multiple tasks within Dataprep.

00:12:10.300 --> 00:12:13.810
Not only can you visually
design the sequence of tasks,

00:12:13.810 --> 00:12:16.750
but you can actually
monitor their execution

00:12:16.750 --> 00:12:19.330
status and their outputs.

00:12:19.330 --> 00:12:23.000
This comes in handy whenever one
flow depends on a [? Urdia ?]

00:12:23.000 --> 00:12:24.880
flow to have run completed.

00:12:24.880 --> 00:12:28.600
Or you need to run a command
statement or a SQL statement

00:12:28.600 --> 00:12:31.040
before running another flow.

00:12:31.040 --> 00:12:34.340
Coming soon, you'll be able
to trigger these plans based

00:12:34.340 --> 00:12:37.640
on various external events,
like the arrival of a new file

00:12:37.640 --> 00:12:40.300
or an external API call.

00:12:40.300 --> 00:12:41.920
Then you can build
conditional logic

00:12:41.920 --> 00:12:46.750
to decide which set of tasks
you want to execute next.

00:12:46.750 --> 00:12:48.610
We also know that
many users already

00:12:48.610 --> 00:12:51.700
have existing data
pipelining tools that

00:12:51.700 --> 00:12:54.070
are used in production
today, so we

00:12:54.070 --> 00:12:56.560
wanted to provide flexibility
with Cloud Dataprep

00:12:56.560 --> 00:12:59.950
to integrate into those
existing workflows.

00:12:59.950 --> 00:13:02.070
This is why we've extended
the work we introduced

00:13:02.070 --> 00:13:05.550
last year with Cloud Composer
and Dataprep's job execution

00:13:05.550 --> 00:13:10.080
APIs by adding brand new APIs
to fetch profiling and recipe

00:13:10.080 --> 00:13:12.460
metadata directly from Dataprep.

00:13:12.460 --> 00:13:14.190
These APIs were
designed so that you

00:13:14.190 --> 00:13:16.230
can leverage key
metrics generated

00:13:16.230 --> 00:13:20.100
from the Dataprep job, such as
data quality results, directly

00:13:20.100 --> 00:13:23.040
into your external tool
through the same workflow

00:13:23.040 --> 00:13:25.430
tool of choice.

00:13:25.430 --> 00:13:26.000
OK.

00:13:26.000 --> 00:13:27.650
We've got one last
category, but maybe

00:13:27.650 --> 00:13:29.730
it's the most important one--

00:13:29.730 --> 00:13:31.380
security and governance.

00:13:31.380 --> 00:13:33.990
We've seen the adoption
Dataprep grow significantly

00:13:33.990 --> 00:13:36.690
across multiple teams
and departments.

00:13:36.690 --> 00:13:39.630
As a result, these teams
have higher demands

00:13:39.630 --> 00:13:41.910
in terms of their security
and governance requirements

00:13:41.910 --> 00:13:44.370
to meet this broader
environment need.

00:13:44.370 --> 00:13:45.870
Security has always
been top of mind

00:13:45.870 --> 00:13:47.850
with Dataprep,
which is why we've

00:13:47.850 --> 00:13:50.940
extended our native
integration with Google IM

00:13:50.940 --> 00:13:53.520
to now add fine
grained data access

00:13:53.520 --> 00:13:57.380
permissions within
the Dataprep project.

00:13:57.380 --> 00:13:59.720
Security has always been
top of mind with Dataprep,

00:13:59.720 --> 00:14:02.450
so building on our native
integration with Google IM,

00:14:02.450 --> 00:14:04.310
we've added
fine-grained data access

00:14:04.310 --> 00:14:07.290
within each Dataprep project.

00:14:07.290 --> 00:14:09.810
Now teams can control
individual data set access

00:14:09.810 --> 00:14:12.270
through a combination
of Google IM roles,

00:14:12.270 --> 00:14:15.830
as well as BigQuery and
Cloud Storage access rights.

00:14:15.830 --> 00:14:19.740
This also applies to G Suite,
where you can import sources

00:14:19.740 --> 00:14:21.300
like Google Sheets.

00:14:21.300 --> 00:14:24.570
Now when a shared Google Sheet
is imported into Dataprep,

00:14:24.570 --> 00:14:27.750
the same access rights that
were granted in G Drive

00:14:27.750 --> 00:14:29.610
are now applied
within Dataprep, which

00:14:29.610 --> 00:14:33.450
means only users who are
authorized to see and prepare

00:14:33.450 --> 00:14:37.160
that data are the ones who
are going to access it.

00:14:37.160 --> 00:14:39.790
Lastly, networking has
been the other major part

00:14:39.790 --> 00:14:41.980
that we've been improving
in terms of security.

00:14:41.980 --> 00:14:44.890
And the goal is to
support a much wider range

00:14:44.890 --> 00:14:46.570
of configurations
that you've set up

00:14:46.570 --> 00:14:48.260
within your IT environment.

00:14:48.260 --> 00:14:52.600
This includes custom networks,
private IPs, shared VPCs.

00:14:52.600 --> 00:14:55.720
So in the latest edition,
you'll see many new options

00:14:55.720 --> 00:14:58.120
that are available when
you run a Cloud Dataprep

00:14:58.120 --> 00:15:00.160
job in Dataflow.

00:15:00.160 --> 00:15:02.770
You've always been able to
run a job in different regions

00:15:02.770 --> 00:15:05.020
from different data
centers around the world.

00:15:05.020 --> 00:15:07.450
But in addition to that,
now you can specify things

00:15:07.450 --> 00:15:11.380
like a custom VPC network name,
sub network, custom service

00:15:11.380 --> 00:15:13.270
names, labels.

00:15:13.270 --> 00:15:16.480
And you can even fine
tune some of the execution

00:15:16.480 --> 00:15:21.931
parameters for the Dataflow
job itself, such as workers.

